---
/**
 * /nuclear-bomb-map/ — the rings on real ground.
 *
 * This page is the only place on the site that loads a map. The homepage keeps
 * its SVG ring diagram: that one survives scripting being off, and the two are
 * not substitutes for each other.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import BombMap from '../components/BombMap.astro';
import { YIELDS } from '../lib/effects';
import type { YieldKt } from '../lib/effects';
import { DEFAULT_CITY, cities, getCity } from '../lib/cities';

const city = getCity(DEFAULT_CITY) ?? cities[0];
const defaultYield: YieldKt = 100;
const defaultBurst = 'air' as const;
---

<Base
  title="Nuclear Bomb Map: Blast Rings on Real Ground, 20 Cities"
  description="Pick a city and a yield and see the rings on real ground, north-up and at true scale, with every distance traced to a printed page of the 1977 manual."
  path="/nuclear-bomb-map/"
  current="map"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script is:inline>
      document.documentElement.classList.add('js');
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <p class="eyebrow">Public-domain source · no model output</p>
  <h1>Nuclear Bomb Map — the rings on the ground, not on a chart</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The same five distances as the <a href="/blast-radius/">blast radius table</a>, drawn where
    they actually land. Pick a city, pick a yield, and the rings are laid over the ground at
    true scale, north-up. Every number behind a ring still carries the figure and printed page
    it came from.
  </p>

  <BombMap city={city} y={defaultYield} burst={defaultBurst} cities={cities} />

  <h2>What the map adds, and what it does not</h2>

  <p>
    A radius in a table is a number you have to imagine. Here the rings are geodesic circles —
    true ground distances — projected onto the basemap, so at 60° of latitude they are visibly
    not the same shape as at the equator. The centre is a gazetteer point, and each ring is
    drawn from that point outward, so what you see is the distance, not a stylised target.
  </p>

  <p>
    What it does not add is any new information about the effects themselves. It does not know
    where buildings are, what they are made of, whether anyone is outdoors, or which way the
    wind is blowing. The manual's distances are to a given overpressure on flat terrain at sea
    level; a city with hills, a shoreline, or a dense core is not flat, and this page makes no
    correction for that. See <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Two things worth knowing before you read the rings</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Burst height is inside the number, not beside it.</strong> Each overpressure ring is
    read at the peak of its own contour, so its burst height differs from its neighbours'. The
    readout under the map gives each row's figure and page; the table gives each row's burst
    height in feet.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The 1 Mt rows leave the chart's stated assumptions behind.</strong> Reading at the
    peak puts the 1 Mt burst heights at 10,174 ft (5 psi) and 15,750 ft (1 psi), while
    Fig.&nbsp;3.73c is computed for a homogeneous sea-level atmosphere at 5,000 ft or less
    (§3.73, p.&nbsp;110). Those two rings are the manual's curve multiplied by the manual's
    scaling law, and should be read as the edge of what the chart supports.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    Yields covered: {YIELDS.map((y) => `${y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt`).join(' · ')}.
    Cities: {cities.length}. Contact bursts are shown for the fireball and the two overpressure
    rings; thermal radii are not drawn for a contact burst, because Fig.&nbsp;7.42 is an air-burst
    chart and the manual handles surface-burst heating by a separate route (§7.41, p.&nbsp;290).
  </p>
</Base>
